---
import Ladder from "../components/Ladder.astro";
import Schedule from "../components/Schedule.astro";
import PageLayout from "../layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import heroDrape from "../assets/images/hero-drape.svg";
---

<PageLayout title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`} description={courseMeta.description} lead={false}>
  <img
    class="hero"
    src={heroDrape.src}
    alt="A green surgical drape, seen from above. Its window frames a patch of skin marked with a dashed red incision line that has not been cut."
  />
  <div class="kicker">
    {courseMeta.code} · Postgraduate · {courseMeta.session}, {courseMeta.year}
  </div>
  <ul class="tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>
  <h1>{courseMeta.title}</h1>
  <hr class="rule" />
  <p class="lede">
    Every other treatment has to beat a placebo. Surgery mostly doesn't. This
    course spends twelve weeks on the operation that makes the comparison
    possible: the one where nothing is done, carefully.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    A sham procedure is not the absence of an operation. It is an operation —
    designed, rehearsed, consented and reviewed — whose only job is to leave
    the real one nothing to hide behind.
  </blockquote>

  <figure>
    <Ladder />
    <figcaption>
      <strong>The Sham Fidelity Ladder.</strong> Introduced in Round 4, used in
      every Round after it. Every rung up buys blinding with risk. Round 7 is
      about who is allowed to make that purchase.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="two">
    <div>
      <h2>What you will make</h2>
      <p>
        By Week 12 you will have written a complete sham-controlled protocol
        for a procedure that is performed today without one. You build it one
        section per Round. Nobody is operated on. The protocol is the
        operation.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Who this is for</h2>
      <p>
        Surgical trainees, trial methodologists, perioperative and research
        nurses, biostatisticians, ethicists, and anyone who has said "well, it
        obviously works" about a procedure and would like to find out whether
        that is true.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Five trials you will know by heart</h2>
  <ul class="trials">
    <li>Moseley et al., 2002 — arthroscopic surgery for knee osteoarthritis</li>
    <li>
      Buchbinder et al. and Kallmes et al., 2009 — vertebroplasty for
      osteoporotic fractures
    </li>
    <li>ORBITA (Al-Lamee et al.), 2017 — PCI for stable angina</li>
    <li>
      SYMPLICITY HTN-3 (Bhatt et al.), 2014 — renal denervation for resistant
      hypertension
    </li>
    <li>
      Freed et al., 2001 — embryonic dopamine-neuron transplantation for
      Parkinson's disease
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will be able to do</h2>
  <p>By the end of the course you will be able to:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Explain why an improvement after surgery is not, by itself, evidence
      that the surgery worked.
    </li>
    <li>
      Take apart a published sham-controlled trial and rate its sham on the
      Sham Fidelity Ladder, with evidence.
    </li>
    <li>
      Design a sham procedure, and a blinding plan around it, for a
      procedure that has never been tested against one.
    </li>
    <li>
      Justify the risk of a sham to an ethics committee, and write a
      consent form a patient can understand without it unblinding them.
    </li>
    <li>
      Decide in advance what your trial's results will mean, and review
      someone else's protocol as a committee member would.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How the course runs</h2>
  <p>
    Each week has one <strong>Round</strong>, on Wednesday. Six weeks also
    have a <strong>lecture</strong>, on Monday, which the Round then puts to
    work. There are no tutorials separate from the Rounds.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every Round adds one section to your protocol. By Week 11 the protocol
    exists, because you have been writing it all semester. Week 12 is its
    defence.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Phase</th>
        <th>Weeks</th>
        <th>What happens</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Pre-op</strong></td>
        <td>1–4</td>
        <td>
          Why surgery was rarely tested against placebo, and what a sham is
          made of. Ends with the Ladder.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Theatre</strong></td>
        <td>5–9</td>
        <td>Building the trial: blinding, outcomes, ethics, consent, stopping rules.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Debrief</strong></td>
        <td>10–12</td>
        <td>What happens after a trial, where shams cannot reach, and the defence.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Assessment at a glance</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Code</th>
        <th>Task</th>
        <th>Weight</th>
        <th>Due</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>A1</td>
        <td><a href={withBase("/assessments/a1-sham-autopsy/")}>Sham Autopsy</a> — rate a published trial on the Ladder</td>
        <td>35%</td>
        <td>Fri 2 Oct (Week 8)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>A2</td>
        <td><a href={withBase("/assessments/a2-the-protocol/")}>The Protocol</a> — your own sham-controlled trial</td>
        <td>50%</td>
        <td>Fri 23 Oct (Week 11)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>A3</td>
        <td><a href={withBase("/assessments/a3-ethics-committee-response/")}>Ethics Committee Response</a> — review a classmate's protocol</td>
        <td>15%</td>
        <td>Fri 30 Oct (Week 12)</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Before you enrol</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Assumed knowledge.</strong> A first course in research methods or
    clinical epidemiology, or equivalent professional experience. You do not
    need to have performed surgery. Most of the teaching team has not.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Workload.</strong> About ten hours a week: two hours of lecture
    and Round, three to four of reading, and the rest on your protocol.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where to start.</strong> Read <a href={withBase("/sessions/week-01-surgical-exceptionalism/")}>Week 1</a>, then the course <a href={withBase("/policies/")}>policies</a>.
  </p>

  <Schedule />

  <p class="foot">No patients will be operated on in this course, sham or otherwise.</p>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .hero {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: clamp(160px, 26vw, 360px);
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center;
    border-radius: 8px;
    margin-block-end: 1.5rem;
  }

  .kicker {
    color: var(--sham-drape);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }

  .tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0.6rem 0 0;
  }

  .tags li {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--sham-drape);
    background: var(--sham-drape-tint);
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 0.15rem 0.65rem;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 5vw, 3rem);
    line-height: 1.08;
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
  }

  .rule {
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--sham-incision);
    width: 7.5rem;
    margin: 1.1rem 0 1.25rem;
    border: 0;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    max-width: 40rem;
  }

  blockquote {
    margin: 2rem 0;
    padding: 1.1rem 1.35rem;
    border-inline-start: 4px solid var(--sham-drape);
    background: var(--sham-drape-tint);
    font-size: 1.2rem;
  }

  figure {
    margin: 2rem 0;
  }

  figure picture {
    display: block;
  }

  figure img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    max-width: 45rem;
    display: block;
  }

  figcaption {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary, #4a5a55);
    margin-block-start: 0.5rem;
  }

  .two {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 1.75rem;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    margin: 2.25rem 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .trials {
    padding-inline-start: 1.25rem;
  }

  .trials li {
    margin: 0.25rem 0;
  }

  .foot {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary, #4a5a55);
    margin-block-start: 2.25rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .two {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
